# Architecture {#architecture}

![The servers and the apps on them](architecture.svg)

## Cluster and high availability {#architecture-ha}

{% if f.nodes | length > 1 %}
The servers form a *cluster*: a group of servers that work as one.
{% else %}
The server runs as a *cluster* of one. A cluster is a group of servers that work as one; more servers can be added later.
{% endif %}
{% if swarm %}
The cluster is run by *Docker Swarm*, which starts each app in a *container* (an app packed with everything it needs) and restarts it when it stops.
{% else %}
The cluster is run by *k3s*, a small edition of Kubernetes. It starts each app in a *container* (an app packed with everything it needs) and restarts it when it stops.
{% endif %}
*Managers* are the servers that steer the cluster. The cluster keeps working while more than half of its managers are up.

*High availability* (HA) means that a part keeps working when one server stops. This is the HA level of each part:

| Part | How it is set up | If one server stops |
|:----------------|:--------------------------------------|:--------------------------------------|
| Cluster managers | {{ managers | length }} manager{{ "s" if managers | length != 1 else "" }}: {{ managers | join(", ") }} | {% if managers | length >= 3 %}The cluster keeps working ({{ (managers | length - 1) // 2 }} may stop){% else %}The cluster cannot be steered until the manager is back{% endif %} |
{% if f.traefik_nodes %}
| Web entry (Traefik) | On {{ f.traefik_nodes | join(", ") }} | {% if f.traefik_nodes | length > 1 %}DNS still points at the stopped server; browsers may try it first{% else %}No web page opens until that server is back{% endif %} |
{% endif %}
{% if f.database %}
| Database ({{ NAME.get(f.database.app, f.database.app) }}) | {% if f.database.ha and f.database.app == "postgres" %}Patroni on {{ app["postgres"].nodes | join(", ") }}, with etcd and a database-proxy{% elif f.database.ha %}Galera on {{ app["mariadb"].nodes | join(", ") }}, with a database-proxy{% else %}One server: {{ runs_on(app[f.database.app]) }}{% endif %} | {% if f.database.ha and f.database.app == "postgres" %}Another server becomes the primary by itself{% elif f.database.ha %}The other two keep taking writes{% else %}The database stops until the server is back{% endif %} |
{% endif %}
{% if f.cache %}
| Cache ({{ NAME.get(f.cache.app, f.cache.app) }}) | {% if f.cache.ha %}Primary and two copies with Sentinel on {{ app[f.cache.app].nodes | join(", ") }}{% else %}One server: {{ runs_on(app[f.cache.app]) }}{% endif %} | {% if f.cache.ha %}Sentinel makes a copy the new primary{% else %}The cache stops until the server is back{% endif %} |
{% endif %}
{% if has("garage") %}
| Storage (Garage) | {{ app["garage"].nodes | length }} server{{ "s" if app["garage"].nodes | length != 1 else "" }}, {{ app["garage"].nodes | length }} cop{{ "ies" if app["garage"].nodes | length != 1 else "y" }} of every file | {% if app["garage"].ha %}Files stay available from the other copies{% else %}Storage stops until the server is back{% endif %} |
{% elif has("rustfs") %}
| Storage (RustFS) | One server: {{ runs_on(app["rustfs"]) }} | Storage stops until the server is back |
{% endif %}

## How a visitor reaches an app {#architecture-traffic}

{% if f.traefik_nodes %}
1. *DNS* (the internet's address book) turns `anything.{{ f.domain }}` into the public address of {{ f.traefik_nodes | join(", ") }}.
2. The browser connects to *Traefik*, the front door, on port 443 (HTTPS). Port 80 only redirects to 443.
3. Traefik checks who may pass. Each page has one *guard*:
   - **admin**: the visitor's address must be on the admin allow-list, then the admin login asks for a password{% if f.admin_sso %} and a code (2FA){% endif %};
   - **admin-ip**: the address must be on the admin allow-list; the app then shows its own login;
   - **public**: anyone may open it; the app has its own login, or none.
4. Traefik sends the visitor to the app, wherever it runs in the cluster.

The admin allow-list (`admin_allow_from`) is {{ anyone(f.admin_allow_from) }}.
{% else %}
Traefik is not installed, so this system has no web pages. Apps are reached through SSH (see [chapter 5](#access)).
{% endif %}

