## How to change a password {#credentials-change}

ansibro keeps a copy of every password in a folder called `state/` on the computer that runs the installer. It uses that copy each time it runs. So a password must be changed in the right place, or the next install undoes it or fails.

{% set classes = people_rows | map(attribute="change") | list %}
{% set unverified = people_rows | rejectattr("verified") | list %}
{% if "file" in classes %}
### File first {#credentials-file-first}

For: {{ people_rows | selectattr("change", "equalto", "file") | selectattr("verified") | map(attribute="title") | join(", ") or "none" }}.
ansibro writes these passwords into the app every time it runs. A change made only in the app is undone at the next install.

1. On the installer computer, put the new password in the file `state/secrets/<name>`.
2. Run `./install.sh`.
3. Update the credentials file, and every app of yours that uses this password.

{% endif %}
{% if "both" in classes or unverified %}
### App and file together {#credentials-both}

For: {{ (people_rows | selectattr("change", "equalto", "both") | map(attribute="title") | list + unverified | rejectattr("change", "equalto", "both") | map(attribute="title") | list) | join(", ") }}.
ansibro logs in with these passwords, or they are needed to restore. If only one place changes, the next install fails.

1. Change the password in the app.
2. At the same time, put the same password in `state/secrets/<name>` on the installer computer.
3. Run `./install.sh`, then the acceptance test (`tests/check.py`).
4. Update the credentials file.

{% if has("mariadb") %}
- **MariaDB root:** the nightly backup uses it. If it changes only in MariaDB, the backup fails without a sound{% if mon == "victoria" %} until the alert BackupTooOld fires a day later{% endif %}.
{% endif %}
{% if f.backup.enabled %}
- **Backup (restic):** run `sudo restic -r <repository> key passwd` on one server, then put the new password in `state/secrets/restic`, then run `./install.sh` (it rewrites `/opt/ansibro/backup/password` on every server). Keep the old password until the new one works: without it, old backups cannot be read.
{% endif %}
{% if unverified %}
- {{ unverified | map(attribute="title") | join(", ") }}: how the app takes a new password was not tested, so treat {{ "it" if unverified | length == 1 else "them" }} as "app and file together".
{% endif %}

{% endif %}
{% if "app" in classes %}
### App first {#credentials-app-first}

For: {{ people_rows | selectattr("change", "equalto", "app") | selectattr("verified") | map(attribute="title") | join(", ") or "none" }}.
The app reads these passwords only the first time it starts. After that the app owns them.

1. Change the password in the app.
2. Put the same password in `state/secrets/<name>`, so the records and the acceptance test stay right.
3. Update the credentials file.

{% if has("authelia") %}
- **Authelia:** the file `users.yml` on {{ f.first_manager }} is the true list of Authelia accounts.
{% endif %}

{% endif %}
### Do not change {#credentials-internal}

The passwords in the group "{{ groups["internal"] }}" are used only between programs. No person types them. Change them only if they leaked, and let the ansibro operator do it.

{% if has("garage") %}
- **Garage:** editing the Garage key file adds a second key; the old key stays valid until it is deleted with the garage command.
{% endif %}

> **Important:** ansibro's own summary file says "change them in the app, then update the file too". That is wrong for the classes "file first" and "app and file together". Follow this chapter instead.

{% if f.admin_sso %}
## Your phone code (2FA) {#credentials-2fa}

The admin login asks for a password and a code from an *authenticator app* on a phone (*2FA*, two-factor login).
{% if f.admin_sso == "authelia" %}
The vendor set this up during the work. Register your own phone:

1. Log in at <https://authelia.{{ f.domain }}/> with the Authelia account.
2. Choose to register a new device. Authelia has no mail server, so it writes the confirmation link to `/opt/ansibro/authelia/config/notification.txt` on {{ f.first_manager }}.
3. An operator reads that file over SSH and gives you the link.
4. Scan the code with your authenticator app.
{% else %}
The code is set up at the first login of the user admin in the Keycloak realm "ansibro". If the vendor logged in first, the vendor's phone is linked:

1. Ask the vendor to remove its authenticator from the user admin in Keycloak.
2. Log in at <https://oauth.{{ f.domain }}/> and scan the new code with your own phone.
{% endif %}
{% endif %}

## Who keeps state/ {#credentials-state}

{% if state_owner == "vendor" %}
**{{ vendor_name }} keeps `state/`** (the installer's copy of the passwords).
After you change a password of the class "file first" or "app and file together", send the new value to the vendor **inside a credentials file with its own passphrase**, never in an email or chat. Otherwise the next install by the vendor undoes your change ("file first") or fails ("app and file together").
{% else %}
**{{ client_name }} keeps `state/`.** The credentials file holds the attachment "ansibro installer files" (`config.yml` and every password file), so your own operator can run ansibro {{ f.ansibro.version or "" }} again.
{% endif %}

## Ending the vendor's access {#credentials-revoke}

Planned date: {{ npdate(a.get("vendor_access_end")) }}. On that date:

1. Remove the vendor's SSH key from `~/.ssh/authorized_keys` of the user `{{ f.ssh.user }}` on every server. ansibro does not manage SSH keys.
2. Remove the vendor's addresses from `ssh.allow_from` and `admin_allow_from` in `config.yml`, then run `./install.sh`.
3. Change the passwords as above.
{% if f.backup.enabled %}
4. Know that old values stay in `/opt/ansibro/backup/installer/state/secrets/` on {{ f.first_manager }} and in older backups.
{% endif %}
5. The vendor deletes its copy of `state/` when you agree it may.

## Changing the passphrase of the credentials file {#credentials-passphrase}

In KeePassXC: menu **Database → Database Security**, then set a new password. (In KeePass 2: **File → Change Master Key**.) Keep a backup copy of the file in a safe place.

